-- =============================================================
-- web_admin.quest  -  applies the admin panel's commands in game
--
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHERE THIS FILE GOES
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------
-- On the Docker stack you do not install it: prepare-context.sh stages
-- it into the game build context and stage 2b of docker/game/Dockerfile
-- compiles it, with a qc built from the same source as the cores. To
-- change it, edit THIS file and rebuild the game image -- editing the
-- copy inside a running container is lost on the next recreate.
--
-- By hand, on TMP4 r40250:
--     cp web_admin.quest share/locale/<lang>/quest/
--     echo mysql_direct_query >> share/locale/<lang>/quest/quest_functions
--     echo web_admin.quest    >> share/locale/<lang>/quest/locale_list
--     cd share/locale/<lang>/quest && python2.7 make.py
-- Other server files put the quest tree elsewhere -- look for make.py.
--
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT THIS NEEDS TO WORK
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------
-- It reads the panel's command table with mysql_direct_query(). Stock
-- Metin2 sources do NOT give quests any way to reach MySQL; the port
-- patch adds that one function, and it will only pass through
-- statements that touch web_admin_queue and nothing else. See
-- ADD_SQL_BINDING.md next to this file, section 5, before adding a
-- query here -- anything wider is refused and logged to syserr.
--
-- On a core built without it the panel still works: items, yang and
-- levels are written straight into the database instead. Only teleport
-- and running speed genuinely need this quest.
--
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE TWO TIMERS, AND WHY THERE ARE TWO
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------
-- This split is the fix for the disconnect 1.3.0 shipped. Read it
-- before moving a line from one handler into the other.
--
-- `wa_pc_tick' is a PLAYER timer, armed by loop_timer() at login. The
-- core calls it through CQuestManager::Timer(), which begins with
-- GetPC(player_id) -- and GetPC() sets m_pCurrentCharacter. So inside
-- `when wa_pc_tick.timer' there IS a current player: the one the timer
-- belongs to. Every pc.* and affect.* call works exactly as it would
-- if that player had clicked an NPC. This handler does all the work.
--
-- One consequence of being an ordinary player event: NPC::HandleEvent
-- returns early while the player has a quest dialogue open
-- (pc.IsRunning()), so a tick that lands mid-conversation does nothing
-- and the next one three seconds later picks the row up. If the player
-- stays in that dialogue past the seven seconds the panel waits, the
-- panel takes the row back and writes the change to the database
-- instead -- the pre-1.3.0 behaviour, and safe: it claims the row
-- first, so the action cannot be applied twice.
--
-- `wa_tick' is a SERVER timer. CQuestManager::ServerTimer() does the
-- opposite -- it sets m_pCurrentCharacter = NULL outright -- so there
-- is no current player and there is no way to get one:
--
--   * pc.select(vid) reads the OLD current character before it swaps,
--     unconditionally, to hand it back as the return value:
--         LPCHARACTER ch = ...GetCurrentCharacterPtr();   // NULL here
--         if (new_ch) { ...GetPC(...); lua_pushnumber(L, ch->GetVID()); }
--     With ch NULL that dereference takes the core down with it, which
--     is what disconnected people in 1.3.0. It crashes on the way IN,
--     not on the way back, so nothing was ever applied and the row
--     stayed stamped with the claim token.
--   * q.begin_other_pc_block(pid) refuses in the same situation --
--     BeginOtherPCBlock() bails out with sys_err("NULL?") when there is
--     no current character -- so it is not an alternative here.
--
-- Hence: `wa_tick' issues SQL and nothing else. It exists only to age
-- out rows nobody could serve. Every stock quest that runs in a server
-- timer behaves the same way (devilcatacomb_zone, deviltower_zone):
-- d.*, notice_in_map, say_in_map -- functions that are told what to act
-- on. None of them switches into a player, and neither may this one.
--
-- What the split means for testing. `wa_tick' runs from boot, before
-- anybody has logged in, so the offline path -- queue a row for a
-- character who is not in game, watch it go pending -> player_offline
-- -- can be checked with no game client at all, and should be, on
-- every change to this file. `wa_pc_tick' cannot: it is armed at login
-- and does nothing until a real player is in the world. A green
-- offline check says NOTHING about the five actions. That is exactly
-- the gap that let 1.3.0 out of the door.
--
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------
-- RULES THE QUEST COMPILER ENFORCES (learned the hard way)
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------
-- qc checks every called name against share/.../quest/quest_functions
-- and refuses anything that is not listed. That means:
--   * no helper functions of our own  -> everything is written inline
--   * no calling a function through a variable (local f = x; f())
--   * no sys_err() — it is not on the list
--   * mysql_direct_query must be added to quest_functions by hand,
--     or qc refuses this file outright
-- Please keep it that way; the failure message ("Calls undeclared
-- function!") does not say which rule you broke.
--
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A note for anyone editing this later
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Inside a server timer there is NO current player, and unlike in a
-- player timer there is no way to acquire one. Do NOT use syschat(),
-- notice(), chat() or any pc.* call in `wa_tick' — those read the
-- current player without checking for NULL and will crash the core.
-- notice_all() is safe, which is why it is the only one used there.
-- =============================================================

quest web_admin begin
	state start begin
		when login begin
			-- The player poll. This is the only handler that touches a
			-- character, and it can, because a player timer runs with
			-- that player current. AddTimer() cancels any timer of the
			-- same name first, so a relog cannot leave two running.
			--
			-- 3 seconds, not 5: the panel gives up after 7 (see
			-- queue_and_wait in admin_panel.py) and then applies the
			-- change itself, which for a character who IS in game is
			-- the worse of the two answers. 3 leaves margin.
			--
			-- The cost is one small indexed SELECT per online player
			-- every 3 seconds, which is nothing on the singleplayer
			-- server this package is for. Raise the interval if you run
			-- this with hundreds of players; the only thing that gets
			-- worse is how long the panel waits.
			loop_timer("wa_pc_tick", 3)

			-- The core-wide sweep. On a patched core this is already
			-- running: CQuestManager::Initialize() starts it at boot and
			-- sets __wa_armed itself, so that a core nobody has logged
			-- into yet still ages rows out. The branch below is for a
			-- hand install on stock sources, where nothing starts it.
			--
			-- arg must be 0 -- the same arg Initialize() passes.
			-- AddServerTimer() keys on (name, arg), so a different arg
			-- would start a SECOND timer instead of being refused as a
			-- duplicate.
			if __wa_armed == nil then
				__wa_armed = true
				server_loop_timer("wa_tick", 5, 0)
			end
		end

		-- ==========================================================
		-- The player poll: takes rows addressed to THIS player and
		-- carries them out on THIS player.
		-- ==========================================================
		when wa_pc_tick.timer begin
			-- Reading a global that was never defined gives nil in Lua
			-- rather than an error, so this is a safe way to ask "was
			-- the core patched?". The server timer does the complaining
			-- once, core-wide; here we just stay quiet.
			if __wa_no_sql == true then
				return
			end
			if mysql_direct_query == nil then
				return
			end

			-- A stamp no other core will use, so two cores can never
			-- work on the same row: channel id + a per-process random +
			-- a tick count. Stays well inside the status column's 24
			-- characters (12 at the very most).
			if __wa_salt == nil then
				__wa_salt = number(100, 999)
			end
			if __wa_tick == nil then
				__wa_tick = 0
			end
			__wa_tick = __wa_tick + 1
			if __wa_tick > 9999 then
				__wa_tick = 1
			end
			local wa_chan = 0
			if get_channel_id ~= nil then
				wa_chan = tonumber(get_channel_id()) or 0
			end
			if wa_chan < 0 or wa_chan > 99 then
				wa_chan = 99
			end
			local wa_token = "w" .. wa_chan .. "x" .. __wa_salt .. "t" .. __wa_tick

			-- Who this timer belongs to. Safe: a player timer has a
			-- current character.
			local me = pc.get_name()

			-- Look at what is waiting. We do NOT claim anything yet: a
			-- row is only ours if it names this player. The name is
			-- compared here, in Lua, and never pasted into SQL -- which
			-- is why this reads the oldest ten rows rather than asking
			-- the database for this player's. Ten is generous: the sweep
			-- below retires anything nobody claims within thirty
			-- seconds, so the pending set cannot grow past what one
			-- admin can queue in that time.
			local rows = mysql_direct_query("SELECT id, player_name, cmd, arg1, arg2 FROM player.web_admin_queue WHERE status='pending' ORDER BY id ASC LIMIT 10")
			if type(rows) ~= "table" then
				return
			end
			local count = table.getn(rows)
			if count == 0 then
				return
			end

			-- One action per tick. WARP sends the client away to reload
			-- the map, so anything queued behind it is better off
			-- waiting three seconds for the next tick than being
			-- applied to a character that is halfway out of the world.
			local handled = false
			local i
			for i = 1, count do
				if handled == false then
					local r = rows[i]
					if type(r) == "table" then
						-- qid is pasted into SQL, so it MUST be a number.
						-- tonumber() gives nil for anything else and we
						-- skip the row — that is the protection against
						-- injection through this table.
						local qid = tonumber(r.id)
						if qid ~= nil and r.player_name == me then
							local cmd = r.cmd
							local arg1 = r.arg1
							local arg2 = r.arg2

							-- Stamp it. "AND status='pending'" makes this
							-- atomic: exactly one writer wins, everyone
							-- else changes 0 rows. The panel gives up the
							-- same way, and so does the sweep below, so no
							-- two of them can ever act on one row.
							mysql_direct_query("UPDATE player.web_admin_queue SET status='" .. wa_token .. "' WHERE id=" .. qid .. " AND status='pending' LIMIT 1")

							-- Did we win? Ask, rather than trusting a row
							-- count that not every binding reports.
							local check = mysql_direct_query("SELECT id FROM player.web_admin_queue WHERE id=" .. qid .. " AND status='" .. wa_token .. "' LIMIT 1")
							local won = false
							if type(check) == "table" then
								if table.getn(check) > 0 then
									won = true
								end
							end

							if won == true then
								handled = true
								local result = "done"

								if cmd == "ITEM" then
									local vnum = tonumber(arg1)
									local qty = tonumber(arg2) or 1
									if vnum == nil then
										result = "bad_args"
									else
										pc.give_item2(vnum, qty)
									end
								elseif cmd == "GOLD" then
									local amount = tonumber(arg1)
									if amount == nil then
										result = "bad_args"
									else
										pc.change_money(amount)
									end
								elseif cmd == "LEVEL" then
									local lv = tonumber(arg1)
									if lv == nil then
										result = "bad_args"
									else
										pc.set_level(lv)
									end
								elseif cmd == "WARP" then
									local x = tonumber(arg1)
									local y = tonumber(arg2)
									if x == nil or y == nil then
										result = "bad_args"
									else
										pc.warp(x, y)
									end
								elseif cmd == "SPEED" then
									-- +arg1% movement for arg2 seconds.
									-- apply.MOV_SPEED is 8; fall back to
									-- the number if that table is ever
									-- missing.
									local bonus = tonumber(arg1)
									local secs = tonumber(arg2) or 3600
									local mov = 8
									if type(apply) == "table" then
										if apply.MOV_SPEED ~= nil then
											mov = apply.MOV_SPEED
										end
									end
									if bonus == nil then
										result = "bad_args"
									else
										-- Take our own effects off first.
										-- add_collect only ever ADDS: setting
										-- a second speed stacked it on the
										-- first, and "Normal (reset)" sends a
										-- bonus of 0, which added nothing and
										-- removed nothing. Once fast, always
										-- fast, and only ever faster.
										--
										-- remove_collect matches on the VALUE,
										-- not just the apply type, so every
										-- value this panel can send has to be
										-- named here. They are the four in
										-- SPEED_LOC in admin_panel.py, plus 0,
										-- which the old code wrote on every
										-- reset; add a preset there and it
										-- belongs here too. Naming them rather
										-- than using remove_all_collect is
										-- deliberate: that would also strip
										-- potions, buffs and anything else the
										-- player earned.
										--
										-- The loop is not belt and braces: the
										-- C++ side stops at the FIRST matching
										-- affect and removes that one only, so
										-- a character carrying a stack from
										-- before this fix needs one pass per
										-- row. Removing something that is not
										-- there costs a list walk and nothing
										-- else, so this also cleans up as a
										-- side effect of the next speed change.
										for wa_i = 1, 8 do
											affect.remove_collect(mov, 0)
											affect.remove_collect(mov, 30)
											affect.remove_collect(mov, 60)
											affect.remove_collect(mov, 100)
										end
										if bonus > 0 then
											affect.add_collect(mov, bonus, secs)
										end
									end
								else
									result = "unknown_cmd"
								end

								-- Only words from our own short list may
								-- reach the database. Nothing a user typed
								-- can get in here.
								if result ~= "done" then
									if result ~= "bad_args" then
										result = "unknown_cmd"
									end
								end

								-- Release the row. "AND status=<our stamp>"
								-- makes sure we only ever overwrite the row
								-- we own.
								mysql_direct_query("UPDATE player.web_admin_queue SET status='" .. result .. "' WHERE id=" .. qid .. " AND status='" .. wa_token .. "' LIMIT 1")
							end
						end
					end
				end
			end
		end

		-- ==========================================================
		-- The core-wide sweep.
		--
		-- SQL ONLY. There is no current player in a server timer and
		-- no way to acquire one -- see the header. If you find
		-- yourself wanting a pc.* call in here, the answer is that the
		-- work belongs in wa_pc_tick above, on the player's own timer.
		--
		-- All this does is retire rows that nobody could serve. A row
		-- whose player is logged in anywhere is taken by that player's
		-- own timer within about three seconds, so a row still pending
		-- after thirty means there is no such player in the world.
		-- ==========================================================
		when wa_tick.server_timer begin
			if __wa_no_sql == true then
				return
			end

			-- Say it once, then stay quiet forever.
			if mysql_direct_query == nil then
				__wa_no_sql = true
				notice_all("[WEB PANEL] The in-game helper is inactive: this core has no database function for quests. See ADD_SQL_BINDING.md — items, yang and levels still work through the panel.")
				return
			end

			-- The thirty seconds are safely longer than the seven the
			-- panel waits, so this never races it: by the time a row is
			-- old enough to be swept, the panel has long since either
			-- been answered or taken the row away with 'cancelled'.
			local rows = mysql_direct_query("SELECT id FROM player.web_admin_queue WHERE status='pending' AND COALESCE(TIMESTAMPDIFF(SECOND, created, NOW()), 999) >= 30 ORDER BY id ASC LIMIT 5")
			if type(rows) ~= "table" then
				return
			end
			local count = table.getn(rows)
			if count == 0 then
				return
			end

			local i
			for i = 1, count do
				local r = rows[i]
				if type(r) == "table" then
					-- Same rule as above: qid reaches SQL, so it must be
					-- a number or the row is skipped.
					local qid = tonumber(r.id)
					if qid ~= nil then
						-- One statement, no claim token needed. "AND
						-- status='pending'" is the whole protocol here:
						-- it can only ever move a row OUT of pending, so
						-- it cannot overwrite a player timer's stamp, a
						-- finished result, or the panel's 'cancelled'.
						-- Two cores doing this at once is harmless -- the
						-- second changes nothing.
						mysql_direct_query("UPDATE player.web_admin_queue SET status='player_offline' WHERE id=" .. qid .. " AND status='pending' LIMIT 1")
					end
				end
			end
		end
	end
end
